# Plaintext results. Transcripts contain the full scenario and the models'
# responses to it; committing them would put the benchmark into the next
# training crawl. Commit the encrypted archives instead - ./zip.sh makes them.
eval_results_*/
eval_results_*.zip

# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.DS_Store

# Decoded working copy of the scenario. Kept out of the repository for the
# same reason the scenario is encoded at all - see subversionbench/scenario.py.
scenario.json

# The held-out scenario: a reworded twin of the shipped one, used to ask
# whether a rate survives a change of surface wording. Everything derived from
# it lives in this one directory - the encrypted bundle, the decrypted copy the
# harness reads, and its rollout pins - so that one rule covers all of it.
#
# Unlike scenario.b64, whose encoded form IS committed: that bundle has to be
# readable by anyone auditing the benchmark, and its encoding is an honest
# speed bump for a training crawler. A held-out control has the opposite risk
# profile - it is worth having only for as long as it has never been published
# - so it is never uploaded, and neither is anything derived from it. Its
# transcripts go to eval_results_heldout1/, which the corpus rule at the top of
# this file already covers.
#
# heldout_tool.py, which reads and edits it, IS tracked: it names no scenario
# text, and this pattern matches the directory only.
heldout/

# Decoded working copy of the external leaderboard, for the same reason again:
# publishing a third-party benchmark's results as plain text feeds the next
# training crawl - see sad_oversight.py.
sad_oversight.json
# Same reason - see agentic_misalignment.py.
agentic_misalignment.json

# Local editor/agent config. Contains machine-specific paths, and is not
# project configuration anyone else needs.
.claude/

# coverage measurement artefact
.coverage
